{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["SAT local search modifies variable assignments while network pruning removes structural elements permanently, changing the search space irreversibly","Removing a comparator alters the network's global behavior on all 8192 test vectors simultaneously, whereas flipping one SAT variable affects only clauses containing that variable","SAT has no inherent size-minimality notion; sorting networks have a proven minimum S(n) that the pruned network may or may not achieve"],"overturn_conditions":["If greedy single-elimination from Batcher's network reaches 45, the SAT-local-optimum analogy's predictive transfer is refuted for this domain","If SAT local search with single flips reliably escapes local optima without coordinated moves, the analogy's foundation weakens"],"shared_structure":["Both start from a known valid solution (Batcher's network / a known SAT satisfying assignment) and attempt to reach a better solution via local modifications","Both have exact polynomial-time verification of candidate solutions (0-1 principle for networks / model evaluation for SAT)","Both may encounter local optima where no single local move (comparator removal / variable flip) yields improvement, yet a multi-move escape exists"],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["If SAT local search with single-flip moves commonly plateaus at local optima that require coordinated multi-flips to escape, then single-comparator elimination from Batcher's network likely plateaus at a local optimum requiring coordinated multi-removals","The empirical observation that SAT restarts and randomization help escape local optima suggests that randomized multi-comparator elimination (not just greedy) might reach 45 even if greedy single-elimination does not"]},"claim":"Systematic comparator elimination from Batcher's 48-comparator odd-even merge network for n=13 will yield at best a 46-comparator network (removing exactly 2 comparators), but cannot reach 45, because Batcher's recursive merge structure creates only pairwise redundancy at n=13 — after removing one comparator from a redundant pair, its partner becomes individually essential. This predicts that greedy single-elimination pruning reaches 46 and that exhaustive pairwise-elimination from the 46-comparator result also plateaus at 46, discriminating against the hypothesis that the known 45-network is reachable by pruning Batcher's construction.","counterconditions":["If single-elimination greedy pruning from Batcher's 48-comparator network reaches 45, the claim is strongly overturned and the pairwise-redundancy hypothesis is false.","If exhaustive pairwise-elimination from the Phase 1 result reaches 45, the claim is overturned — pairwise redundancy is deeper than predicted.","If triple-elimination (removing 3 comparators simultaneously) from the Phase 1 result reaches 45, the specific pairwise-redundancy claim is false but the broader hypothesis that pruning Batcher's cannot reach 44 may still hold.","If a different initial construction (e.g., bitonic sort or shell-sort-based) prunes to 45 while Batcher's does not, the claim is narrowed to Batcher's structure specifically but the general elimination-from-known-construction approach is not refuted."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"680b4c459d28bf7a","quote":"S(n) denotes the minimum comparator count of any n-input sorting network. As compiled in this dossier:"},{"block":"8a7a7d8f89f48b3e","quote":"S(n) is PROVEN for n up to 12"},{"block":"6050cf69f391fa33","quote":"Consequently a 13-input candidate is verified by running all 2^13 = 8192 binary vectors through it"},{"block":"2c7a5cc0d7461dd5"}],"mechanism":"Construct Batcher's odd-even merge sort network for n=13 using the standard recursive odd-even merge procedure, yielding 48 comparators. Phase 1 (single-elimination): For each of the 48 comparators, remove it and test the 47-comparator network by running all 2^13=8192 binary vectors from {0,1}^13 through it via the 0-1 principle. Greedily remove all individually-removable comparators, arriving at a locally irreducible network of predicted size 46. Phase 2 (pairwise-elimination): From the Phase 1 result, exhaustively test all C(m,2) pairs of simultaneous removals, each verified by the 0-1 principle. Predicted result: no pair is simultaneously removable. Phase 3 (control): If Phase 2 somehow reaches 45, test all pairwise removals from the 45-network to probe for 44. The discriminating prediction is that Phases 1 and 2 both yield 46, not 45.","optional_refs":["src-c684127f981a8a8c1d5e0739c804e68436eebc3d"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-c684127f981a8a8c1d5e0739c804e68436eebc3d"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55}],"simulation_proposals":[]}